WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:17.265
(clapping)

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.173
- Welcome, welcome everybody.

00:00:26.551 --> 00:00:28.550
I'd love to welcome you
here this afternoon to

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.310
New Haven’s Shubert Theater.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.533
To this conversation with Wendell Berry.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.080
My name's Mark Bomford and I'm the

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.430
Director for the Yale
Sustainable Food Project.

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.753
This is a long awaited visit.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.730
Since the Yale Sustainable
Food Project's inception

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.430
10 years ago Wendell Berry's writings

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.290
have appeared prominently
on course reading lists

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.350
and quotations from his writings

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.180
have opened more essays
and graced the final slides

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.940
of more PowerPoint presentations

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.910
than I think Mr. Berry would
be comfortable knowing about.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.590
(laughing)

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:06.420
Students who study food and
agricultural topics here at Yale

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.120
soon learn that if you are
ever struggling to articulate,

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.130
some essential idea about the fundamental

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.320
interconnectedness of it all,

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:18.150
how nature and culture and health and soil

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.760
and character and economy
are not really objective

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.420
separate entities, then you
turn to Berry's writings.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:28.290
You can be confident that you will

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:31.770
find somewhere in his work
exactly what you're looking for,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.990
and will be stated with absolute

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:36.063
mental fog lifting lucidity.

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:40.130
So it's no surprise that one of the most

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.050
frequently asked questions I have

00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:44.310
received since I arrived here was,

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.440
do you think that we could
ever get Wendell Berry

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.180
to come speak at Yale?

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.624
And until a month ago, my
answer had always been,

00:01:51.624 --> 00:01:53.459
it's been tried before, you know,

00:01:53.459 --> 00:01:56.670
many people have asked him
many many times before,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.340
and he almost never leaves
his farm, and that is true.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.560
So, given that I had basically
written this whole thing off,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.210
the chance of ever getting
Wendell Berry to come and visit,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.501
you can imagine just how excited I was

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:13.080
in the last couple of weeks
how a wonderful team of people

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:14.370
and thank you all,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.290
mobilized to make this
whole thing a possibility,

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:20.670
and this included the discovery,
in this whole process,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:24.241
that we actually happen to have
a Yale College Master here,

00:02:24.241 --> 00:02:26.330
who was a native Kentuckian

00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.660
and a lifelong Wendell Berry fan.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.980
And that college Master was Jeff Brenzel,

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:33.890
who was able to extend the
offer of a Chubb Fellowship

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.540
to Mr. Berry through handwritten letters

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.807
sent by the U.S. Postal Service
back and forth to Kentucky.

00:02:39.807 --> 00:02:42.160
(laughing)

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.410
The Chubb Fellowship
was founded with a gift

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.240
from Yale Alumnus Hendon
Chubb, and since 1949,

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.230
has been Yale's most prestigious honor

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.560
conferred on visiting speakers.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:56.340
The Chubb Fellowship is
devoted to encouraging interest

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.760
and devotion to public service.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:00.530
Chubb fellows spend their time at Yale

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.380
in close informal contact
with members of the community,

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:06.380
and also make an appearance
open to the general public.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.430
Which I guess is happening right now.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.410
Former Chubb Fellows include
five U.S. Presidents,

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:13.280
and many other heads of state,

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.430
including authors such as Octavio Paz,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.633
Carlos Fuentes, and Toni Morrison,

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:22.690
and many other nationally
and internationally prominent

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.080
citizens and leaders.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.560
And it's the Chubb
Fellowship that allows us

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.850
to bring this afternoons
conversation between

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:31.240
Wendell Berry and two of Yale's own,

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:33.880
Jeff Brenzel, and Mary Evelyn Tucker,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.170
and I'll introduce both of them in turn.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:40.770
Jeff is a 1975 graduate of Yale College.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.390
He made an early career
in nonprofit management,

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.430
including organizations in
his home state of Kentucky,

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.620
which he shares with Wendell Berry.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.090
In his mid career, he embarked
on a PhD in moral philosophy

00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:55.190
at the University of Notre
Dame, while at the same time,

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.950
founding and developing
InterLearn Incorporated,

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.610
and invest a backed
venture that used new media

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.090
and technologies to
produce career education

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.340
and liberal arts programs
for adult learners.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.970
Jeff returned to Yale in 1997
as the Executive Director

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:12.270
of the Association of
Yale Alumni, or the AYA.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:15.660
And in September 2005, he was named the

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:17.830
Dean of Undergraduate Admissions,

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:21.130
and served in this position for
eight years until past June,

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.530
which probably makes him
the bravest man I know.

00:04:24.428 --> 00:04:25.261
(laughing)

00:04:25.261 --> 00:04:28.960
In April of 2010, Jeff was
appointed to a concurrent

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.060
five term year as the Master,

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.520
that's a five year term rather,

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.430
as the Master of Timothy Dwight College,

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:37.460
which is one of Yales 12
undergraduate residential colleges

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.170
which also makes him a steward of

00:04:40.175 --> 00:04:41.784
the Chubb Fellowship Endowment,

00:04:41.784 --> 00:04:43.870
which brings Wendell Berry here today.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.570
Jeff holds an appointment as a lecturer

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.460
in the humanities department at Yale,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.350
and teaches in directed studies,

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:52.113
Yales intensive humanities
program for freshmen.

00:04:52.113 --> 00:04:55.267
His research interests center
around the history of ideas

00:04:55.267 --> 00:04:57.090
about human nature and the relationship

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:59.453
between the individual and common good.

00:05:00.564 --> 00:05:05.564
Mary Evelyn Tucker, she met
Wendell Berry some 30 years ago,

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.590
when he came to Columbia
University for a dialogue

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:10.730
with her teacher Thomas Berry,

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:12.660
who shared Wendell's
critique of our present

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.012
industrial destruction of land and people.

00:05:16.012 --> 00:05:19.050
Mary Evelyn edited
Thomas Berry's critiques

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.640
in numerous books of his essays,

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.384
ranging from Dream of the
Earth to the Great Work.

00:05:24.384 --> 00:05:27.030
As a response to the call by Wendell Berry

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.550
for an affection for place

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.270
and Thomas Berry for a new story of place,

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.200
she and her husband, John Grim,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.640
were executive producers
for a film called,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.500
Journey of the Universe,
which won an Emmy.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:41.410
They also created the forum on religion

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:44.350
and ecology at Yale, which
has published 10 volumes,

00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.190
bringing the worlds religious
traditions into dialogue

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:50.148
with our most pressing
ecological challenges.

00:05:50.148 --> 00:05:53.538
John and Mary Evelyn are senior lecturers,

00:05:53.538 --> 00:05:54.730
and research scholars at Yale,

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:56.910
and direct the MA program
in religion and ecology

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.470
between the School of Forestry
&amp; Environmental Studies,

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.430
and the Yale Divinity School.

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:04.060
And she is on the advisory
board of Orion Magazine

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.500
with Wendell Berry, and that of course

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:08.610
brings me to Wendell Berry,

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:09.820
who probably needs no introduction,

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:11.750
but I'm going to give him one anyways.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:14.210
Wendell Berry is a poet, a novelist,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.240
a philosopher, an environmental activist,

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.865
cultural critic and
importantly to us, a farmer.

00:06:19.865 --> 00:06:23.420
A pioneering and influential
advocate for change,

00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.580
Berry has spent more than 50 years

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:26.990
helping to shape the movements for

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:30.580
agricultural and
ecological sustainability.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.920
His poetry and essays flow from the rich

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.160
agrarian tradition of American writing,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.860
and Berry's relationship to Kentucky farm

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.970
has been compare to that
of the rose to the forest,

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:42.830
a place that nurtures his thinking

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.660
about the value of physical labor,

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.140
self sufficiency, and
communities of people

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.920
living in harmony with the natural world.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:51.800
Berry has garnered international respect

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:54.780
and admiration for all
aspects of his life's work,

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.060
including winning the T.S. Elliot Award,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.050
the 2000 Poets prize,
the Thomas Merton Award,

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:02.470
the Aiken Taylor Award for
Modern American Poetry,

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.677
and the National Humanities Medal,

00:07:04.677 --> 00:07:07.408
among many, many, many others.

00:07:07.408 --> 00:07:08.900
He was inducted into

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:12.853
the American Academy of
Arts and Sciences in 2013.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.930
A prolific author, Berry has
generated dozens of novels,

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.940
short stories, poems, and essays,

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.623
since publishing his first novel in 1960.

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:24.267
In Berry's visit today,

00:07:24.267 --> 00:07:26.170
he expressed a particular interest

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:28.318
in learning about the Yale farm,

00:07:28.318 --> 00:07:30.990
and the students engaged
in study there saying,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.949
it's good that outfits
like yours can have a farm,

00:07:33.949 --> 00:07:36.010
and I'd like to see what it can give

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:37.983
and what encouragement I can give it.

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.290
Mr. Berry will begin with a short reading,

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:45.090
after which we will
start the conversation.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.530
Ladies and gentlemen,
please join me in welcoming,

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.427
Jeffrey Brenzel, Mary Evelyn
Tucker, and Wendell Berry.

00:07:52.427 --> 00:07:54.677
(clapping)

00:07:59.292 --> 00:08:00.246
- Thank you.

00:08:00.246 --> 00:08:01.352
- It's all yours.

00:08:01.352 --> 00:08:03.098
- Thank you.

00:08:03.098 --> 00:08:05.348
(clapping)

00:08:23.939 --> 00:08:28.595
Well, I know a privilege when I see one.

00:08:28.595 --> 00:08:29.428
(laughing)

00:08:29.428 --> 00:08:31.250
Or partially see one.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:34.383
And I thank you, thank you very much.

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:40.270
To start things today I'm going to read

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.793
a fragment of a speech,

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:48.250
and I don't apologize much
for it's being a fragment

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:51.370
because we deal in fragments you know,

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:53.673
it's impossible to get it all.

00:08:55.210 --> 00:09:00.210
So this is just a way to
begin our discussion today.

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:06.803
The industrial economy
from agriculture to war,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:11.500
is by far the most violent
the world has ever known,

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:15.003
and we all are complicit in it's violence.

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:18.260
The history of industrialization

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:21.020
has been violent from the start,

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:23.183
as the Luddites quickly learned.

00:09:24.170 --> 00:09:27.458
The purpose of labor saving technology

00:09:27.458 --> 00:09:32.120
has always been to cheapen
work by displacing workers,

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:34.700
thus increasing the flow of wealth

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:37.643
from the less wealthy to the more wealthy.

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:44.310
It is a fact, one we have
never adequately acknowledged

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:48.950
or understood, that at
the end of World War II,

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:53.060
industry geared up to adapt the mechanical

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:57.430
and chemical technologies
of war to agriculture,

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.233
and other ways of using land.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:05.760
At the same time, certain
corporate and academic leaders,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:10.360
known collectively as the
Committee for Economic Development

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.163
decided that there were too many farmers.

00:10:17.702 --> 00:10:22.702
The relatively self sufficient
producers on small farms

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:27.950
needed to become members of
the industrial labor force

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:30.793
and consumers of industrial commodities.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.770
Reducing the number of farmers and farms

00:10:34.770 --> 00:10:39.083
became a devastatingly
effective national policy.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.710
The first problem of a drastic reduction

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.650
of the land using population,

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:50.823
is to keep the land producing
in the absence of the people.

00:10:51.930 --> 00:10:54.710
The Committee for Economic
Development and their allies

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:57.490
were fully aware of this problem,

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.751
and they had a ready solution.

00:10:59.751 --> 00:11:03.610
The absent people would be replaced

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.510
by the mechanical and
chemical technologies

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:10.650
developed for military
use and subsisting upon

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:15.400
a seemingly limitless
bounty of natural resources,

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:19.033
mainly ores and fuels.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.820
Agriculture would become an industry,

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.540
farms would become factories,
like other factories,

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:31.103
evermore automated and
remotely controlled.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:35.020
Industrial land use became a front

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:38.173
in a war against the living world.

00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:42.300
And so with a few exceptions,

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:45.293
the free market was
allowed to have it's way.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:50.850
Finally nearly all of
the land using population

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.064
have left their family
farms and their home places

00:11:54.064 --> 00:11:57.626
and moved or commuted into the cities

00:11:57.626 --> 00:12:02.626
to be industrially or professionally
employed or unemployed.

00:12:05.235 --> 00:12:09.470
And to be entirely dependent on the ways

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:12.123
and the products of industrialism.

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:15.560
This process of eliminating the

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:19.370
too many farmers still continues.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:23.250
Nobody ever said how many were too many.

00:12:23.250 --> 00:12:28.250
Nobody ever said how many
might be actually necessary.

00:12:31.110 --> 00:12:34.110
Even so, to remove the
farmers from farming

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:36.370
required a shift of interest.

00:12:36.370 --> 00:12:39.490
From husbanding the fertility of the land

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:41.720
to burning the fossil fuels,

00:12:41.720 --> 00:12:45.070
with consequences so far more,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:48.703
so far less famous than terrifying.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:53.620
But there was another problem
that the population engineers

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:58.123
did not recognize then, and
have not recognized yet.

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:01.420
Agricultural production without

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:04.453
land maintenance leads to exhaustion.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:10.670
Land that is in use, if
the use is to continue,

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:15.880
must be used with care, and
care is not and can never be

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:21.603
an industrial product
or an industrial result.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:25.460
Care can come only from what we

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:29.610
used to understand as the human heart.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:34.253
So called because it is
central to human being.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:40.830
The human heart is informed
by the history of care

00:13:41.010 --> 00:13:45.350
and the need for care,
also by the heritage

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:49.563
of the skills of caring
and of care taking.

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:54.310
The replacement of our
displaced rural families

00:13:54.310 --> 00:13:59.020
by technologies derived from warfare,

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:02.700
has involved inevitable
a supposedly acceptable

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:07.593
and generally accepted violence
against land and people.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:13.290
By it we established an analogy
between land use and war

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:18.040
that has remained remarkably
consistent ever since.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:22.690
The common theme is a terrible
pragmatism that grants

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:27.690
an automatic predominance
of the end over the means.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.900
The sacrifice of land and people

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:35.900
to the objective of victory,
domination, security, or profit

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:43.920
in oblivion or defiance of
any moral or natural law

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.913
that may stand in the way,

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:50.970
all of our prevalent forms of land use

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:54.963
which is to say, land minus care,

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:00.350
which is to say land use minus care,

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:03.290
produces in addition
to commercial products,

00:15:03.290 --> 00:15:05.773
massive waste and destruction.

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:11.170
War is politics minus neighborly love

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.790
plus technological progress,

00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:18.753
which makes it ever more massively
wasteful and destructive.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.794
There is in fact no significant difference

00:15:23.794 --> 00:15:27.970
between the mass destruction of warfare

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:32.970
and the massive destruction
of industrial land abuse.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:37.300
In order to mine a seam of
coal in eastern Kentucky

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:40.990
and West Virginia, we destroy a mountain,

00:15:40.990 --> 00:15:44.290
it's top soil, and it's forest,

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.970
with no regard for the ecosystem,

00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:51.853
or for the people downhill,
downstream, and later in time.

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:56.480
The difference between
explosion and the coal fields,

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:59.860
and erosion in the corn and soybean fields

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.963
is only that erosion is slower.

00:16:03.970 --> 00:16:07.690
The end, the exhaustion of natures

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.730
life supporting systems is the same.

00:16:13.556 --> 00:16:15.806
(clapping)

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.610
- Wendell, we wanna pick up
on some of those comments

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:34.633
that you've just made and,

00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:38.340
you've been farming for
decades and writing about

00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:40.980
the loss of small family farms since your

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:44.990
path breaking book in 1977,
the Unsettling of America,

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:48.900
and if anything, this
unsettling has intensified

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.910
as more and more people
have moved off the land

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:54.810
and more small family farms are abandoned.

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:59.040
So my question to you
is how does this loss

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:02.600
of a sense of place, farming in community,

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:06.600
living in community, how
does this loss contribute

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:09.270
to the ability that you've mentioned here,

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:13.620
of corporations to strip
mine mountains in Appalachia?

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:17.340
Some of the oldest
mountains on our continent,

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:20.800
or to frack into the Marcellus
Shale in Pennsylvania

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:22.480
and in many parts of the country,

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:26.100
thus destroying
ecosystems, water supplies,

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:28.433
and ways of life for thousands of people?

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:36.050
- Putting places entirely at risk,

00:17:36.050 --> 00:17:40.823
well the coal fields are
a special case in a way,

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:44.431
because the mineral rights were bought

00:17:44.431 --> 00:17:46.963
from people who didn't know what they had,

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:51.970
with money that they really didn't

00:17:51.970 --> 00:17:53.960
know much about the value of.

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:57.200
So, coal rights were bought for

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:58.903
as little as 50 cents an acre.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:04.520
And they were bought under
the old broad form deed,

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.130
that permitted the mining company

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:09.963
to have access to the mineral.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:15.890
That deed was interpreted by the courts

00:18:17.077 --> 00:18:21.490
as the right to overturn
everything on top of the coal

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.250
in order to get at the coal
without any compensation

00:18:24.250 --> 00:18:26.673
to the people who owned what was on top.

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:32.520
So, the separation between
the people and the land

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:35.343
began with the purchase
of those old deeds.

00:18:42.852 --> 00:18:47.852
The history of the
separation between the people

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:50.093
and the farm land is,

00:18:52.740 --> 00:18:54.233
more complicated,

00:18:57.500 --> 00:18:59.463
but let's see.

00:19:00.580 --> 00:19:02.143
How has it happened?

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.673
It happened with,

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:12.940
the help, maybe first of
all, of the depreciation,

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:17.203
of the social regard for farming.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:21.870
People had learned to
put just in front of the

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:25.093
name of their occupation,
I'm just a farmer.

00:19:26.070 --> 00:19:28.930
One of the most disconcerting
things I've heard in my life

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:32.300
at public meetings is to hear
perfectly intelligent people

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:36.920
stand up and say I'm just a farmer, but.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:40.133
And then say something really worthwhile.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.060
So you have that.

00:19:44.060 --> 00:19:48.010
The wish to get away from it.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:53.010
But you also have working against
that whole connection the,

00:19:58.746 --> 00:20:03.560
the difficulty, the adversity
of the farming economy.

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:08.560
The farmer has always been the

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:16.200
last to profit, if indeed
he could profit at all.

00:20:19.290 --> 00:20:24.157
And so we had farmers
telling their children,

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:32.290
get outta here, go to college, don't farm,

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:36.053
my own grandmother told me,
honey don't you ever farm.

00:20:37.144 --> 00:20:37.977
(laughing)

00:20:37.977 --> 00:20:40.700
And that was because my grandfather,

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:45.260
who started to farm in about 1890,

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:49.690
experienced the farm
depression of the 90's,

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:54.690
the depression of the first
decade, of the next century,

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:56.970
the depression of the 20's,

00:20:56.970 --> 00:20:59.860
which started as an agricultural,

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.420
which was an agricultural depression

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:06.730
which went on to become
the depression of the 30's.

00:21:06.730 --> 00:21:10.420
So by the time my grandfather
was as old as I am,

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:14.040
he'd lived through 40 years
of economic adversity,

00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:18.053
had barely held on to the land.

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:24.600
So those are not circumstances
that encourage great love,

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:28.940
and great attentive affection between

00:21:31.610 --> 00:21:35.570
the people and their land.

00:21:35.570 --> 00:21:37.860
To have the land cared
for you have to have

00:21:38.988 --> 00:21:41.888
enough people to care for it,
who know how to care for it,

00:21:42.965 --> 00:21:44.220
and who want to care for it,

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.073
and who can afford to care for it.

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:51.983
Is that still the answer to your question?

00:21:52.856 --> 00:21:54.783
(laughing)

00:21:54.783 --> 00:21:58.870
- Well just to pick up before
we go to the farm bill,

00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.470
I think people here would like to know

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:03.800
what you did in Frankfurt
in the governors office

00:22:03.800 --> 00:22:07.400
to protest the mountaintop removal.

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:11.547
- Well I've been a very misfortune
civil disobedient person.

00:22:13.615 --> 00:22:16.070
(laughing) Civil dis-obeyer.

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:19.020
I've tried it three times and
I never did get put in jail,

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:24.020
which (laughing), which is
a very very satisfactory

00:22:26.060 --> 00:22:28.403
situation as far as I'm concerned.

00:22:29.323 --> 00:22:34.323
We were gonna risk
arrest in a demonstration

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:36.720
organized mainly by Bill McKibben

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:39.190
several years ago in Frankfurt,

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:41.715
and we were marching along and somebody

00:22:41.715 --> 00:22:45.347
shoved a microphone at me and
said do you wanna go to jail?

00:22:45.347 --> 00:22:47.002
And I said hell no!

00:22:47.002 --> 00:22:48.822
(laughing)

00:22:48.822 --> 00:22:49.850
But there's a big difference between

00:22:49.850 --> 00:22:52.970
going to jail and being
willing to go to jail.

00:22:52.970 --> 00:22:55.800
Well the episode that Mary
Evelyn's talking about

00:22:57.130 --> 00:23:02.130
began as a determination that we,

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:05.633
a realization that talking
wasn't gettin' us anywhere.

00:23:07.716 --> 00:23:08.720
That we were the easiest people

00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:11.513
in the state of Kentucky
to ignore, in Frankfurt.

00:23:15.113 --> 00:23:17.696
And we decided that we would go

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:23.720
and move in on the governor
and refuse to get out,

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:27.310
until he did something,
gave us some concessions

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:29.393
about mountaintop removal.

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:33.690
And so we did.

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:38.480
And we told him we were
not much inclined to leave.

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:40.123
And we told him why.

00:23:42.335 --> 00:23:47.335
And he outsmarted us,
he invited us to stay.

00:23:47.458 --> 00:23:49.708
(laughing)

00:23:51.488 --> 00:23:54.600
Which, resulted in one of the finest

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:59.500
weekends I've ever spent
(laughing), in my life.

00:24:00.663 --> 00:24:03.995
'Cause there were about,
I guess 25 or 30 of us

00:24:03.995 --> 00:24:05.595
in the governors reception room.

00:24:07.073 --> 00:24:11.450
And, people were sending
us bedding and food,

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:14.433
and two people even came
in and gave us massages.

00:24:15.907 --> 00:24:18.090
(laughing)

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:21.613
Somebody in Tampa sent us
a great stack of pizzas.

00:24:23.210 --> 00:24:24.183
But we had,

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.860
the loveliest time, we had all this food

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:32.520
out on tables out in the carter
outside the governors office

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:36.150
and we'd invite the security people

00:24:36.150 --> 00:24:39.680
to come and have food
with us, and they did.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:44.020
And we had a very friendly time,

00:24:44.020 --> 00:24:49.020
and we'd made an effort to look good,

00:24:50.410 --> 00:24:54.963
and behave ourselves well,
and clean up after ourselves.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:59.930
And we were people who
already loved each other,

00:24:59.930 --> 00:25:02.580
and we came out of that
loving each other a lot more.

00:25:05.370 --> 00:25:07.420
It was a great time.

00:25:07.420 --> 00:25:12.420
We told stories, and you know,
these damned cell phones,

00:25:13.474 --> 00:25:16.540
you had to be very careful what you said,

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:19.570
and I don't know what I
finally wound up saying

00:25:19.570 --> 00:25:23.560
because I would forget,
but they live streamed it,

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:25.417
as they say, and (laughing).

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:32.310
But we got press interviews,

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:35.990
we really did make very
good use of the time.

00:25:35.990 --> 00:25:40.720
And when we left that office was clean,

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:42.093
I mean it looked like we'd,

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:47.857
we picked up every strand
of lint individually.

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:53.393
So it wasn't long after that,

00:25:55.130 --> 00:26:00.120
well I got an award,
and the Attorney General

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:01.670
made the mistake of telling me that

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:04.173
if I needed anything,
his door was always open.

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:07.000
(laughing)

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.750
Wrote me a letter, and I wrote back,

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:11.210
as a matter of fact there is something,

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:14.860
and so a bunch of us
went back and talked to

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:16.120
not the Attorney General,

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:20.233
we wanted to know what
our legal standing was.

00:26:21.798 --> 00:26:25.420
And so we went back, and
ever since we'd been there,

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:27.300
there'd been somebody every Thursday

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:30.270
occupying the governors reception room.

00:26:30.270 --> 00:26:33.490
And so we occupied it, we
took our turn that day.

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:35.463
And the doors all began to open,

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:38.040
the governors door never opened,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:42.160
but his staff, the doors
of his staff opened,

00:26:42.160 --> 00:26:47.160
and his secretaries, and his
own private state policemen,

00:26:48.650 --> 00:26:50.350
they were all delighted to see us.

00:26:50.350 --> 00:26:54.120
They came out to shake our
hands and welcome us back,

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:56.493
so it was a good thing.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:27:01.170
- Well Wes, Jackson is such a
good friend of yours as well,

00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:04.803
and the two of you have the
best time together, the humor,

00:27:05.915 --> 00:27:10.800
the exchange, but I wanted
to pick up with you and Wes,

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:15.210
because your decades long
of thinking about the land,

00:27:15.210 --> 00:27:16.960
thinking about farming issues,

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:20.420
and Wes and his Land Institute in Kansas

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:23.300
has been talking about
this perennial agriculture.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:24.732
- That's right.

00:27:24.732 --> 00:27:27.110
- And trying to reverse the
annual agriculture, right?

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:32.040
It's 20% perennial, 80% annuals, so--

00:27:32.040 --> 00:27:33.060
- Reverse some of it.

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:37.639
- Yes reverse some of it,
so you two have cooked up

00:27:37.639 --> 00:27:39.563
among others, this 50-Year Farm Bill.

00:27:41.011 --> 00:27:42.470
Would you tell us a little bit about that?

00:27:42.470 --> 00:27:43.853
What is it hoping to do?

00:27:49.141 --> 00:27:52.070
- What we're trying to do in land use

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:58.130
is move from a market
determined, a market determined,

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.520
technologically limited
form of agriculture,

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:06.920
to a form of agriculture that is based on

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:12.903
the instruction of the local ecosystem.

00:28:14.990 --> 00:28:19.990
And one of natures laws that,
as Albert Howard laid it down,

00:28:20.730 --> 00:28:25.730
and I don't think it
was unknown at the time,

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.150
one of the things that
nature insists upon,

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:31.913
and always done herself,
is keep the land covered.

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:37.673
And she does this with perennial plants.

00:28:38.840 --> 00:28:43.840
In nature, according to natures use,

00:28:43.880 --> 00:28:47.773
annual plants are emergency equipment.

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:53.460
She sends in the annuals to occupy

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:57.313
and cover things like
landslides, calamities,

00:28:59.560 --> 00:29:02.590
and we've based our whole agriculture

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:07.163
on the emergency principle.

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:13.260
Annuals die in the fall and
their roots become flimsy

00:29:13.260 --> 00:29:15.043
and lose their power to hold.

00:29:17.306 --> 00:29:21.940
And so the 50-Year Farm Bill, in short,

00:29:26.310 --> 00:29:29.903
proposes to start where we are now,

00:29:30.857 --> 00:29:35.857
with an agriculture
using 80% annual plants

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:41.370
and 20% perennials, and in 50 years,

00:29:41.890 --> 00:29:46.890
by a gradual movement that's
laid out in the farm bill,

00:29:48.167 --> 00:29:52.980
it isn't very long, to reverse that ratio,

00:29:52.980 --> 00:29:57.980
to 80% perennial, and 20% annual.

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:03.843
And this is addressed, this
is an attempt to address,

00:30:05.084 --> 00:30:08.470
I think a pretty credible
attempt to address

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:13.180
the actual problems of
agriculture, soil erosion,

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:17.973
toxicity, and the ruin
of rural communities,

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:23.060
or as we sometimes say
the cultures of husbandry,

00:30:23.060 --> 00:30:26.002
which have pretty well
been wiped out actually.

00:30:26.002 --> 00:30:30.890
Rural communities now are just
little groups of city people,

00:30:30.890 --> 00:30:32.823
watching television and going to town.

00:30:35.486 --> 00:30:37.736
(laughing)

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:41.973
Shopping.

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:48.380
So that's it in a nutshell.

00:30:48.380 --> 00:30:53.380
And perennials, I will say, are pastures,

00:30:56.424 --> 00:30:59.960
hayfields, or fields
of perennial hay crops,

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:05.630
the clovers, alfalfa, timothy,
those things that over winter

00:31:06.560 --> 00:31:11.430
and also the perennial grain crops

00:31:11.430 --> 00:31:14.793
that the Land Institute has
been working on now for,

00:31:15.870 --> 00:31:18.490
35 years I guess.

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:23.490
And they have now produced,
they're the first,

00:31:24.890 --> 00:31:26.623
perennial grain crop.

00:31:28.366 --> 00:31:30.043
It's highly bred,

00:31:35.065 --> 00:31:36.177
grass--

00:31:38.878 --> 00:31:40.070
- This huge rooting grass.

00:31:40.070 --> 00:31:41.470
- Yes, it's a deeply rooted,

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:45.400
well all the perennial roots go deeper

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.153
then the annual roots.

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:53.577
But this is a plant that
they've named Kernza.

00:31:55.320 --> 00:31:58.070
It's an intermediate wheat grass

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:02.040
that has been bred for
productivity, seed production,

00:32:02.040 --> 00:32:06.750
and it actually has been
developed in Kansas,

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:08.960
but it grows better in
Minnesota they've found,

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.060
so there's a fairly significant anchorage,

00:32:12.060 --> 00:32:17.060
90 or so, gonna be grown
in Minnesota this year.

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:20.099
And in five or six years it'll

00:32:20.099 --> 00:32:23.473
be ready to market to farmers.

00:32:25.413 --> 00:32:27.095
(clapping)

00:32:27.095 --> 00:32:30.670
- Yes, this is, so just to conclude,

00:32:30.670 --> 00:32:34.030
we're gonna see Wes at New Year's time,

00:32:34.030 --> 00:32:36.890
so we'll give him a good
report of your visit to Yale,

00:32:36.890 --> 00:32:40.330
but this is a huge,
decades long collaboration

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:41.690
with Wendell Berry and Wes,

00:32:41.690 --> 00:32:44.640
and it does deserve our
gratitude and applause.

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:46.230
- Alright then, you'll have to tell him,

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:48.030
that I've said right here in
front of all these people,

00:32:48.030 --> 00:32:52.550
we gotta put a cap on the
use of high density carbon.

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:54.530
- Yes, that's true.

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:56.361
(laughing and clapping)

00:32:56.361 --> 00:32:58.549
You are so right.

00:32:58.549 --> 00:33:02.990
- I signed a pledge that I would
mention it, so there it is.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:04.290
(laughing)

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:05.837
- I'll report it.

00:33:05.837 --> 00:33:06.683
- Peace be unto Wes.

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:09.900
- Is there anything else that
you're obligated to get in

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:11.787
that you wanna get in right now?

00:33:11.787 --> 00:33:12.782
- Not to mention.

00:33:12.782 --> 00:33:14.470
(laughing)

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:17.540
- Well I can just testify to this which is

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:21.785
if Wendell looks to you
like somebody that would be

00:33:21.785 --> 00:33:23.610
good to hang around the house with

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:26.470
and swap stories, I can
tell you that he is.

00:33:26.470 --> 00:33:29.523
And if you ever get the
chance, you should take it.

00:33:31.970 --> 00:33:36.970
Wendell, you did a lot of
teaching in your earlier days,

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:42.083
and many of us here in the room
think of you as our teacher.

00:33:44.502 --> 00:33:49.400
And (laughing) maybe
it's even for that reason

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:51.210
that you're often quite skeptical

00:33:51.210 --> 00:33:53.363
in your writings about universities.

00:33:54.593 --> 00:33:57.276
(laughing)

00:33:57.276 --> 00:33:59.697
So here we are, at one of these outfits.

00:33:59.697 --> 00:34:01.640
(laughing)

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:02.473
And,

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:07.843
I'd like to ask you just
to explore it a little bit.

00:34:07.843 --> 00:34:11.369
What to you is the upside and the downside

00:34:11.369 --> 00:34:16.130
of university education
as you've experienced it

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:17.343
and you know it in
relationship to the things

00:34:17.343 --> 00:34:19.530
that you care about the most?

00:34:19.530 --> 00:34:21.350
- Well of course the upside is it

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:25.160
puts these young people in a place

00:34:25.160 --> 00:34:27.750
where there are books and teachers,

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:31.390
and gives them that time to

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:35.006
read and figure and write,

00:34:35.006 --> 00:34:40.006
with actually more leisure
than they think they've got.

00:34:40.008 --> 00:34:42.258
(laughing)

00:34:43.170 --> 00:34:45.420
(clapping)

00:34:46.269 --> 00:34:48.673
- [Mary] That's great.

00:34:48.673 --> 00:34:50.920
- When I was in college, I
thought I was about at capacity

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:53.697
and I think I've been
busier every year since.

00:34:53.697 --> 00:34:55.947
(laughing)

00:34:58.013 --> 00:34:59.900
This is a good thing.

00:34:59.900 --> 00:35:03.970
Since we've spoken of
Wes, Wes told me one time,

00:35:03.970 --> 00:35:07.860
he had been to a university and he said,

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:10.070
I walked around that campus,

00:35:10.070 --> 00:35:14.760
and I saw a library full of books,

00:35:14.760 --> 00:35:19.720
and classrooms with blackboards and seats,

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:24.050
and laboratories with wonderful equipment,

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.990
and I said to myself,

00:35:25.990 --> 00:35:29.346
this would be a hell of a
good place to have a school.

00:35:29.346 --> 00:35:31.596
(laughing)

00:35:33.454 --> 00:35:35.287
- [Mary] That's great.

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:48.449
- There's no question that it's

00:35:48.449 --> 00:35:51.210
a great opportunity to go to college,

00:35:51.210 --> 00:35:55.580
but I have taught in
colleges, universities,

00:35:57.410 --> 00:36:01.923
and I have taken a considerable interest,

00:36:04.472 --> 00:36:07.890
and one thing that I know
about the educational system

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:10.750
is that it really is geared toward

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:14.963
an indoor sedentary culture.

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:18.930
It really is saying to these young people,

00:36:18.930 --> 00:36:21.140
especially the ones that
come in off the farm,

00:36:21.140 --> 00:36:26.140
you need to be sittin'
down somewhere, at a desk,

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:28.973
with a salary, and so on.

00:36:30.470 --> 00:36:34.093
You can drop that old
dependence on the weather,

00:36:35.200 --> 00:36:40.200
that old anxiety about the
heavens, and the clouds.

00:36:40.620 --> 00:36:45.620
And that's a great salesman line actually,

00:36:50.430 --> 00:36:52.443
to people who wanna go up in the world,

00:36:54.404 --> 00:36:59.100
but what it's done is
excerpt a lot of people

00:36:59.100 --> 00:37:02.950
from communities where
they ought to have stayed

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:06.330
or gone back to to help.

00:37:06.330 --> 00:37:08.470
And I think it's been a long time

00:37:08.470 --> 00:37:11.830
since even the colleges of agriculture

00:37:11.830 --> 00:37:16.830
were consciously preparing
young people to go home,

00:37:17.350 --> 00:37:21.840
and take part and be of
use to their old neighbors.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:24.830
So along with my observation

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:28.670
of universities in my participation,

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:33.670
I have seen this decline of communities.

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:36.840
And it's happening everywhere,

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:38.830
it's happening in the city
just as it is in the country.

00:37:38.830 --> 00:37:40.530
The neighborhoods are breaking up.

00:37:42.868 --> 00:37:45.030
To a considerable extent that's

00:37:46.265 --> 00:37:47.553
for want of local leadership.

00:37:48.696 --> 00:37:53.696
What young people in
educational institutes are

00:37:54.801 --> 00:37:59.023
supposed to be getting, the
capacity to be critical.

00:38:00.006 --> 00:38:05.006
Which I would say is the first
requirement for leadership,

00:38:06.698 --> 00:38:08.915
the capacity to be suspicious

00:38:08.915 --> 00:38:10.850
of what's offered from the outside.

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:13.913
So that isn't happening, but,

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:18.183
another thing that I've
been increasingly aware of,

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:24.210
is that the questions
that are of most concern

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:31.970
can't be answered in any
department, in any one department,

00:38:32.510 --> 00:38:35.310
so I think that the structure,

00:38:35.310 --> 00:38:37.140
the intellectual structure that's

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:42.140
represented by universities is
probably wrong, awry somehow.

00:38:46.676 --> 00:38:50.800
It would be interesting to just
raise a question like this,

00:38:50.800 --> 00:38:52.223
where are we?

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:58.440
And see how long you could
keep that in the walls

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:01.670
of one department or one college.

00:39:01.670 --> 00:39:04.470
First thing you know you'd
have to be calling in people.

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:08.360
You started that in geography,

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:10.730
first thing you know need a historian,

00:39:10.730 --> 00:39:14.030
then a chemist, and a forester,

00:39:14.030 --> 00:39:19.030
and an agronomist, and even
maybe the english department.

00:39:20.291 --> 00:39:22.541
(laughing)

00:39:23.490 --> 00:39:26.573
So, so this becomes real when you say

00:39:28.370 --> 00:39:33.370
not just where are we,
but what's here with us?

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.003
Who are our neighbors here?

00:39:37.587 --> 00:39:41.540
And what's happened here?

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:44.930
And what should have happened here?

00:39:44.930 --> 00:39:47.763
And what should been happening here now?

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:54.520
You see, that keeps
calling in the expertise

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:58.500
from different departments.

00:39:58.500 --> 00:40:02.560
And so I think the
departmentalization probably

00:40:02.560 --> 00:40:04.260
is gonna eventually,

00:40:04.260 --> 00:40:06.683
oh I don't know anything about the future,

00:40:09.020 --> 00:40:11.079
I just call it into question,

00:40:11.079 --> 00:40:12.523
I think that it needs to be questioned.

00:40:13.691 --> 00:40:17.730
It's been oversold,
education's been oversold,

00:40:17.730 --> 00:40:20.340
and in a lot of places, I'm sure not here,

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:22.510
but in a lot of places (laughing),

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:25.203
in a lot of places it's
been underperformed.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:28.060
- Discipline-all-a-tree.

00:40:28.900 --> 00:40:31.150
(clapping)

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:34.510
- So it makes me wanna ask a quick

00:40:34.510 --> 00:40:36.563
follow up question Wendell which is,

00:40:38.210 --> 00:40:42.400
I know that you have
an interest in forms of

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.980
local knowledge, particularly
with respect to land use

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:49.660
and response to the land that you have

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:51.636
a strong feeling about that there's

00:40:51.636 --> 00:40:54.560
forms of education that
should be taking place

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:58.160
outside of colleges and universities.

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:00.653
And I wonder if you might
say a word about that?

00:41:02.310 --> 00:41:06.993
- Well yes, limiting education
to school is a mistake,

00:41:08.320 --> 00:41:13.027
because, well, if children have

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.320
freedom to play in the
landscape for instance,

00:41:17.320 --> 00:41:21.440
they're beginning their education there,

00:41:21.440 --> 00:41:24.240
they're beginning their education at home.

00:41:24.240 --> 00:41:27.360
And maybe we're losing control of

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:29.300
what they're learning at home,

00:41:29.300 --> 00:41:30.493
but they're learning.

00:41:32.209 --> 00:41:37.209
And a lot has been said
about adult education,

00:41:37.675 --> 00:41:42.160
and the importance of it,
and I think that's real,

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:47.160
but to keep somebody learning,
right on through their lives,

00:41:48.829 --> 00:41:51.340
requires keeping 'em aware of

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:55.270
being ignorant all through their lives.

00:41:55.270 --> 00:41:58.093
And so,

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:03.433
we're defining the
problems, again, too simply.

00:42:06.110 --> 00:42:07.723
If you're aware,

00:42:08.720 --> 00:42:11.780
at my age say, of how ignorant you are,

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:13.770
you know that you're not gonna

00:42:13.770 --> 00:42:15.830
remedy that in any simple way,

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:18.283
and you're not gonna ever
completely remedy it.

00:42:21.440 --> 00:42:25.727
- So, I've got another topic that

00:42:27.010 --> 00:42:30.023
I've been anxious to
raise with you which is,

00:42:31.920 --> 00:42:34.130
for almost every one who's come here,

00:42:34.130 --> 00:42:38.840
I know to see you today, these
people in this room know you

00:42:38.840 --> 00:42:43.840
and know your work, and
I think there's probably

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:47.810
hardly a person here with us today

00:42:47.810 --> 00:42:51.728
who hasn't felt encouraged
or inspired or moved

00:42:51.728 --> 00:42:56.728
or personally touched by
something that you had to say.

00:42:57.300 --> 00:43:00.580
You've had the gift of that,
and you've worked at it.

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:02.580
Nobody can say you haven't worked at it.

00:43:03.846 --> 00:43:08.846
And somehow, you've
managed to stay motivated,

00:43:10.146 --> 00:43:12.290
and you've sought to motivate others

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:16.350
to address what seem like, to
most of us, most of the time,

00:43:16.350 --> 00:43:18.423
as overwhelming problems,

00:43:19.750 --> 00:43:23.540
things that are just too
big to get your arms around.

00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:26.768
And when you and I originally
talked on the telephone

00:43:26.768 --> 00:43:29.620
about your visit here, I asked you how you

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:33.890
sustained a sense of optimism about that.

00:43:33.890 --> 00:43:38.384
And you corrected me, you said well,

00:43:38.384 --> 00:43:41.973
you'd be a fool to be an optimist,

00:43:43.260 --> 00:43:45.331
and also that tends to make you think

00:43:45.331 --> 00:43:47.550
that the problem's gonna be solved

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:51.030
and you don't really have to
put out any effort to do it.

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:54.033
And you said that you've lived on hope.

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:58.803
And that hope is a very
different thing than optimism.

00:43:59.650 --> 00:44:01.920
And I was moved by that conversation

00:44:01.920 --> 00:44:03.810
and I hoped you might explain

00:44:05.154 --> 00:44:07.443
that distinction for everybody here.

00:44:08.610 --> 00:44:13.310
- Well optimism and
pessimism are programs.

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:15.620
It means that you've already
decided ahead of time

00:44:15.620 --> 00:44:18.060
how things are gonna come out,

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:23.060
and you've in effect taking
possession of the future,

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:26.920
in the name of optimism or pessimism,

00:44:26.920 --> 00:44:31.920
and you've reduced the future in a way to

00:44:33.560 --> 00:44:36.463
your sense of how it is going to turn out.

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:42.053
Hope is a different matter,

00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:46.683
because hope is,

00:44:48.710 --> 00:44:50.833
well hope is the opposite of despair.

00:44:51.725 --> 00:44:53.120
(laughing)

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:55.343
As you know, and,

00:44:59.290 --> 00:45:04.290
so if you think it's
important to stay at work

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:07.200
and if you believe in
the possibility of good

00:45:07.200 --> 00:45:11.040
and sustaining work, work
that will sustain you

00:45:11.040 --> 00:45:14.563
and help to sustain others and the world,

00:45:16.160 --> 00:45:21.160
then you have to define
the ground of hope.

00:45:23.410 --> 00:45:25.690
And this is work for the present,

00:45:25.690 --> 00:45:29.260
this is not making up a
version of the future,

00:45:29.260 --> 00:45:32.250
something you're gonna work for later.

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:34.660
It's something that
you've got to look around

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:36.713
and justify it now.

00:45:37.930 --> 00:45:41.790
And what justifies it for one thing

00:45:41.790 --> 00:45:45.853
is the reality of good possibilities.

00:45:47.890 --> 00:45:52.890
Examples that show that
a better of forestry,

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:56.183
a better way of farming is possible.

00:45:58.696 --> 00:46:01.290
A better way of community life,

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:03.003
a better way of family life.

00:46:04.630 --> 00:46:07.530
You look around and you begin to have

00:46:07.530 --> 00:46:12.360
your little lexicon of good examples,

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:14.690
and that helps like everything.

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:18.210
If you thought that something
better was not possible,

00:46:18.210 --> 00:46:21.840
you had no way to believe
in that possibility,

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:23.523
you'd just be in awful shape.

00:46:26.350 --> 00:46:30.550
Another thing I think is to
be able to make in yourself

00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:35.550
and perfectly as necessary
the sense of the possibility

00:46:36.070 --> 00:46:37.783
that you can do better.

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:41.303
This always lasts me about two days.

00:46:42.780 --> 00:46:45.030
(laughing)

00:46:50.732 --> 00:46:54.110
I have this oppressive
sense that I can come

00:46:54.110 --> 00:46:58.045
and visit Yale for two days
and behave pretty well.

00:46:58.045 --> 00:46:59.771
(laughing)

00:46:59.771 --> 00:47:02.981
And I better hurry away on the third day.

00:47:02.981 --> 00:47:06.040
(laughing)

00:47:06.040 --> 00:47:09.613
But yes sometimes, I have, it seems to me,

00:47:10.490 --> 00:47:13.060
done work that did some good

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:15.973
and didn't do any damage into the bargain.

00:47:17.803 --> 00:47:19.513
And I like knowing that.

00:47:23.610 --> 00:47:25.773
It gives me hope.

00:47:27.940 --> 00:47:32.860
Having other people who are your comrades,

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:34.843
your friends, your allies,

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:38.633
who have hope helps you have hope.

00:47:40.300 --> 00:47:43.663
And to have in mind your predecessors,

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:49.398
who you know, some of
'em you've read about,

00:47:49.398 --> 00:47:52.410
some of 'em maybe as times goes on

00:47:52.410 --> 00:47:55.113
you actually know, have known.

00:47:56.870 --> 00:48:00.190
Who have kept open that possibility

00:48:00.190 --> 00:48:02.123
that people could live with hope.

00:48:04.690 --> 00:48:05.723
That's hope giving.

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:09.023
Hope lives on hope.

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:15.780
There's a lot to say about it,

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:19.210
I'm trying to think up maybe two

00:48:19.210 --> 00:48:21.013
more percent of the rest of it.

00:48:22.310 --> 00:48:25.690
- Maybe I can pick up on this theme.

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:30.352
There's a wonderful few lines
among many wonderful few lines

00:48:30.352 --> 00:48:33.140
that struck me, but this one is,

00:48:33.140 --> 00:48:37.220
knowledge without
affection leads us astray,

00:48:37.220 --> 00:48:42.220
affection, leads us by way of
good work to authentic hope.

00:48:42.460 --> 00:48:46.070
So my question here is what is affection?

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:48.130
You speak about it so often.

00:48:48.130 --> 00:48:51.270
And you encourage us to develop informed,

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:54.290
practical, and practiced affection.

00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:55.863
Which I think is marvelous.

00:48:57.430 --> 00:49:01.092
- Well, we're getting
into the intangibles now.

00:49:01.092 --> 00:49:01.925
- Right.

00:49:04.169 --> 00:49:06.209
- And I'm really (laughing),

00:49:06.209 --> 00:49:07.603
I'm really interested in the intangibles.

00:49:09.069 --> 00:49:12.353
I'm interested in the economic
results of the intangibles.

00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:18.880
Things like affection,
familiarity, loyalty,

00:49:21.890 --> 00:49:25.493
those things, neighborliness.

00:49:27.280 --> 00:49:31.258
Kindness, loving kindness
towards your neighbors.

00:49:31.258 --> 00:49:35.313
Those things have an economic result.

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:41.098
People raised in the kind of
Protestantism I was raised in

00:49:41.098 --> 00:49:43.463
don't know that but the
Amish know it perfectly.

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:48.203
Well, affection, we all feel it.

00:49:50.254 --> 00:49:55.254
One word for it's love, which
is a badly depreciated word,

00:49:56.599 --> 00:49:59.640
and we got to watch it,
it's a word you mustn't use

00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:03.363
except in context, some kind of context.

00:50:04.870 --> 00:50:09.410
I mean, your arms around
somebody is a context.

00:50:09.410 --> 00:50:11.660
(laughing)

00:50:17.390 --> 00:50:19.690
People who are falling
in love feel affection.

00:50:21.242 --> 00:50:22.940
And we know what to make of that.

00:50:24.150 --> 00:50:25.700
That raises a lot of questions.

00:50:26.770 --> 00:50:31.770
If you like it, and you like the person

00:50:31.990 --> 00:50:35.150
that it's happening to you with,

00:50:35.150 --> 00:50:37.720
then another very demanding question,

00:50:37.720 --> 00:50:39.153
how can you make it last?

00:50:40.730 --> 00:50:43.363
How can you make it come to a good result?

00:50:45.254 --> 00:50:46.903
And this brings a lot into play.

00:50:48.150 --> 00:50:51.130
And a lot of intangibility's

00:50:51.130 --> 00:50:56.033
that are gonna have very tangible results.

00:50:57.250 --> 00:51:02.250
So affection, well, I'm
beginning to think of affection

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.693
and knowledge almost as synonyms.

00:51:08.580 --> 00:51:11.220
- But and that's the difference
between universities,

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:13.500
which keep them separate, right?

00:51:13.500 --> 00:51:17.540
- Well it is, well you have
these ideas cropping up

00:51:17.540 --> 00:51:21.013
like objectivity, which most
people know is worthless.

00:51:22.629 --> 00:51:24.879
(laughing)

00:51:26.699 --> 00:51:27.782
- Absolutely.

00:51:37.354 --> 00:51:42.354
- You're going to learn
about the things you love.

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:50.480
And maybe the reverse is true sometimes.

00:51:50.610 --> 00:51:54.143
Sometimes the things you know,
you find out are not worthy.

00:51:55.890 --> 00:52:00.890
But when you love the woods,
you have the best motivation

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:03.873
for learning something about forestry.

00:52:04.970 --> 00:52:07.810
- Wendell, if I could just
pick up on that point,

00:52:07.810 --> 00:52:11.760
because what strikes me, and
I'm sure many many others,

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:14.550
is exactly that, your love of the woods,

00:52:14.550 --> 00:52:17.440
your love the farm, your love of place,

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:20.100
and I just wanted to read these few lines

00:52:20.100 --> 00:52:23.250
of where you're moving into a wooded area,

00:52:23.250 --> 00:52:26.160
and you're in a timelessness.

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:29.234
And just have you reflect for us this

00:52:29.234 --> 00:52:32.040
incredible mingling of life

00:52:32.040 --> 00:52:33.950
and death that takes place there.

00:52:33.950 --> 00:52:36.710
This is your magnificent language, but,

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:39.060
ruin is in place here,

00:52:39.060 --> 00:52:42.120
the dead leaves rotting on the ground,

00:52:42.120 --> 00:52:47.120
the live leaves in the air are
gathered in a single dance,

00:52:47.180 --> 00:52:49.849
that turns them round and round.

00:52:49.849 --> 00:52:54.520
The fox cub trots his
almost pathless path,

00:52:54.520 --> 00:52:57.460
as silence as his absence.

00:52:57.460 --> 00:53:02.420
These passing's resurrect
a joy without defect,

00:53:02.420 --> 00:53:07.420
the life that steps and
sings in ways of death.

00:53:11.630 --> 00:53:14.327
- Well I think that's pretty good stuff.

00:53:14.327 --> 00:53:15.889
- [Mary] Yeah.

00:53:15.889 --> 00:53:18.139
(laughing)

00:53:23.740 --> 00:53:27.140
Wendell, anyone who can
sing in joy and death

00:53:27.140 --> 00:53:29.680
gives us such encouragement.

00:53:29.680 --> 00:53:32.410
- Well it's all apart of the same thing

00:53:32.410 --> 00:53:34.913
is what I was saying there, but,

00:53:36.545 --> 00:53:41.545
that sort of begins to hint
at this business of affection,

00:53:42.130 --> 00:53:45.403
and what it really is,

00:53:46.511 --> 00:53:50.930
what you find out, if you love the fields,

00:53:53.030 --> 00:53:54.720
and the woods, and you keep going back,

00:53:54.720 --> 00:53:58.210
and you keep looking, I've
been looking at my place now,

00:53:58.210 --> 00:54:00.183
really ever since I was born.

00:54:02.074 --> 00:54:05.003
And what I know is that
it's inexhaustible.

00:54:06.480 --> 00:54:08.633
And it's inexhaustibly beautiful.

00:54:09.790 --> 00:54:14.573
And the beauty of it includes
by now for me a lot of sorrow.

00:54:15.410 --> 00:54:18.450
I can't look at my home landscape without

00:54:18.450 --> 00:54:22.563
noticing that a lot of people
are not there, whom I miss.

00:54:25.121 --> 00:54:27.500
But it becomes rich in that sense.

00:54:27.500 --> 00:54:28.333
And

00:54:32.840 --> 00:54:34.363
so you,

00:54:36.262 --> 00:54:39.920
I get to needing less and less
to go and see other places.

00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:43.873
Because I know I'll never
finish seeing this one place.

00:54:44.760 --> 00:54:47.190
It's inexhaustibly beautiful,

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:49.710
it's inexhaustibly interesting,

00:54:49.710 --> 00:54:52.523
it's inexhaustibly surprising.

00:54:55.080 --> 00:54:57.990
Every day there's the
chance you're gonna see

00:54:57.990 --> 00:54:59.740
something you've never seen before.

00:55:03.630 --> 00:55:08.630
My trapper friend was
telling me the other day

00:55:09.140 --> 00:55:14.140
he'd seen an otter keeping his fish,

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:16.930
he'd caught a pretty good fish,

00:55:16.930 --> 00:55:19.920
and the eagles were after him for it.

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:23.403
And he was a man who's
watched and watched.

00:55:24.910 --> 00:55:27.760
The eagles were really giving
this otter a rough time,

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:30.735
he couldn't stay under
water long enough to get,

00:55:30.735 --> 00:55:32.800
to make 'em give up.

00:55:32.800 --> 00:55:34.763
He finally came up in a pile of brush.

00:55:36.240 --> 00:55:37.373
And that stopped 'em.

00:55:38.320 --> 00:55:39.190
(laughing)

00:55:39.190 --> 00:55:41.300
He was delighted you see,

00:55:41.300 --> 00:55:43.373
and he's been watching a long time.

00:55:47.360 --> 00:55:48.193
- So,

00:55:51.680 --> 00:55:53.830
most of us know you the way that we can,

00:55:53.830 --> 00:55:55.830
which is through your writing of course,

00:55:57.060 --> 00:56:02.060
and you've found poetry and fiction

00:56:03.460 --> 00:56:05.630
have been things that nourished your life

00:56:05.630 --> 00:56:07.590
as an activist and a farmer,

00:56:07.590 --> 00:56:10.170
and you found that the
activist and the farmer

00:56:10.170 --> 00:56:12.303
has nourished the writing.

00:56:15.008 --> 00:56:18.513
Could you just talk a
little bit about that?

00:56:19.644 --> 00:56:22.320
You've had two kinds of
work really in your life,

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:25.670
and you've gone back
and forth between them,

00:56:25.670 --> 00:56:29.040
and I'm curious about how
that movement works for you

00:56:29.040 --> 00:56:30.190
and how it takes place.

00:56:32.550 --> 00:56:36.563
- Actually Thurow saw
that interconnection,

00:56:38.210 --> 00:56:42.260
that the life of the body served

00:56:42.260 --> 00:56:44.403
directly the life of the mind.

00:56:47.300 --> 00:56:52.300
But we're getting me into trouble here,

00:56:53.256 --> 00:56:55.169
because this is hard to talk about.

00:56:55.169 --> 00:56:58.880
I know that my work as a writer has been

00:57:00.082 --> 00:57:02.510
under the influence of
my life as a farmer,

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:05.030
sometimes in obvious ways.

00:57:05.030 --> 00:57:07.233
I've never farmed for a living,

00:57:08.660 --> 00:57:12.270
and nobody could make a
living on the farm I have.

00:57:12.270 --> 00:57:15.283
It's too, it's got too many problems.

00:57:16.720 --> 00:57:18.907
It's either steep or it overflows.

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:27.830
So it's a farm that can teach you a lot,

00:57:27.830 --> 00:57:29.980
but it's not gonna make
you a lot of money.

00:57:31.130 --> 00:57:33.380
(laughing)

00:57:34.430 --> 00:57:36.697
But nevertheless,

00:57:36.697 --> 00:57:41.697
I have put myself in a
situation in which I have to

00:57:44.399 --> 00:57:46.623
go outside everyday to care for livestock.

00:57:48.147 --> 00:57:53.147
I've had to look at what rain
does to uncovered ground,

00:57:53.460 --> 00:57:56.200
I've had to feel the satisfaction

00:57:56.200 --> 00:58:01.050
of watching it fall on ground
that is adequately covered.

00:58:01.050 --> 00:58:04.590
That has informed what I've
written about agriculture,

00:58:04.590 --> 00:58:08.620
it seems to me, with some authority,

00:58:08.620 --> 00:58:11.470
that I wouldn't have
been able to give my work

00:58:11.470 --> 00:58:14.653
if I hadn't been doing it everyday.

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:20.870
And it's also informed my
work with the lively interest

00:58:22.620 --> 00:58:24.170
in the subject that comes from

00:58:24.170 --> 00:58:26.733
the pleasure of taking from it.

00:58:28.361 --> 00:58:33.361
Dread and difficulty, but also pleasure.

00:58:34.880 --> 00:58:37.230
There's some kind of pleasure connected

00:58:37.230 --> 00:58:38.600
with cleaning up a mess.

00:58:39.901 --> 00:58:42.422
(laughing)

00:58:42.422 --> 00:58:44.373
That everybody ought to be aware of.

00:58:46.000 --> 00:58:49.463
The best example I know is washing dishes.

00:58:52.020 --> 00:58:54.720
I'm like anybody else, I
don't do it unless I have to.

00:58:56.534 --> 00:59:01.410
But once I'm in it, I
really love washing dishes.

00:59:01.410 --> 00:59:03.979
You can't imagine, the worst mess,

00:59:03.979 --> 00:59:05.810
there's a bunch of dishes
piled over here on this side,

00:59:05.810 --> 00:59:10.293
dirty and dripping, and ugly,

00:59:11.382 --> 00:59:13.592
(laughing)

00:59:13.592 --> 00:59:17.100
in utter disorder, and you
put 'em through this process

00:59:17.100 --> 00:59:21.890
of soap and scrubbing and
scrubbing and rinsing,

00:59:21.890 --> 00:59:24.790
and all the sudden here they
are over here in a neat pile.

00:59:26.240 --> 00:59:27.809
- Miraculous.

00:59:27.809 --> 00:59:28.642
- All pleasant to look at.

00:59:29.677 --> 00:59:32.873
And it's the same way with
fixing a balled up wire fence.

00:59:34.495 --> 00:59:35.328
I've done it over and over again,

00:59:35.328 --> 00:59:37.700
sometimes in the rain,
sometimes in cold weather

00:59:37.700 --> 00:59:38.933
when my hands were numb.

00:59:40.765 --> 00:59:44.770
To start with a snarl
of wire and work it out

00:59:44.770 --> 00:59:47.800
to something that will
again hold your livestock

00:59:47.800 --> 00:59:49.783
is just so satisfying.

00:59:52.030 --> 00:59:54.960
And there must be some
analogy between that

00:59:54.960 --> 00:59:59.960
and working from this conglomeration,

01:00:02.099 --> 01:00:03.799
this wad of stuff you know that's,

01:00:05.854 --> 01:00:07.823
that's going on inside
your head all the time,

01:00:11.879 --> 01:00:14.179
to making it into something
orderly on a page.

01:00:15.730 --> 01:00:19.360
Now that doesn't mean that what you've got

01:00:19.360 --> 01:00:21.640
on the page is ever adequate to

01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:24.010
what you've got in your mind.

01:00:24.010 --> 01:00:27.493
This is something I've
had to learn to accept,

01:00:28.528 --> 01:00:32.790
first with some regret, and
then with a certain joy,

01:00:32.790 --> 01:00:36.120
that everything we could put into words,

01:00:36.120 --> 01:00:39.283
or numbers, is reduced.

01:00:40.460 --> 01:00:43.850
We're always working and
to the extent that we can

01:00:43.850 --> 01:00:46.103
work and communicate to each about it,

01:00:47.160 --> 01:00:52.160
we're working with a reduced
replica of the reality

01:00:53.420 --> 01:00:56.403
that we're really living
in and experiencing.

01:00:57.550 --> 01:01:01.300
That's why we've got to have
freedom of speech and thought

01:01:02.407 --> 01:01:05.150
of those things which we've
always got to correct.

01:01:05.150 --> 01:01:07.250
People who are by nature reductions

01:01:09.486 --> 01:01:11.493
have got to be correcting all the time.

01:01:14.552 --> 01:01:15.730
- You know, just to pick up the activism

01:01:15.730 --> 01:01:17.370
that Jeff has brought into play,

01:01:17.370 --> 01:01:19.100
I think it's important to mention that

01:01:19.100 --> 01:01:22.140
your paths have crossed
several times in Washington

01:01:22.140 --> 01:01:23.470
with our former dean of the

01:01:23.470 --> 01:01:26.490
School of Forestry and
Environmental Studies, Gus Speth,

01:01:26.490 --> 01:01:29.340
especially on the Exxon pipeline

01:01:29.340 --> 01:01:30.720
with the tar sands in Alberta,

01:01:30.720 --> 01:01:33.230
but I think you spent a
weekend with him in jail,

01:01:33.230 --> 01:01:34.508
in Washington D.C.--

01:01:34.508 --> 01:01:35.506
- No I didn't.

01:01:35.506 --> 01:01:36.639
- Oh, okay.

01:01:36.639 --> 01:01:38.610
(laughing)

01:01:38.610 --> 01:01:40.271
- Nope, I'm sorry.

01:01:40.271 --> 01:01:42.490
(laughing)

01:01:42.490 --> 01:01:44.272
- Bill McKibben's likes--

01:01:44.272 --> 01:01:45.368
- A little.
- That story.

01:01:45.368 --> 01:01:49.670
- No, I didn't even go to
Washington on that jaunt.

01:01:49.670 --> 01:01:51.087
- On that one, okay.

01:01:51.087 --> 01:01:53.451
Was it earlier, one of the coal--

01:01:53.451 --> 01:01:54.850
- I missed my chance.

01:01:54.850 --> 01:01:56.960
(laughing)

01:01:56.960 --> 01:01:59.970
No, I didn't do that,
but I have met Gus Speth,

01:01:59.970 --> 01:02:01.320
I've talked with him, sure.

01:02:02.553 --> 01:02:07.553
- You know, what you said
about that work and writing,

01:02:08.990 --> 01:02:12.873
it just puts me in mind
of things that I've read,

01:02:14.464 --> 01:02:18.620
about the many times through
your writings that you recall,

01:02:18.620 --> 01:02:21.198
including one I sent
out to everybody here,

01:02:21.198 --> 01:02:23.070
what it was like to cut tobacco.

01:02:23.070 --> 01:02:25.420
Now I've cut tobacco,
and it's not an easy job.

01:02:27.195 --> 01:02:30.323
But there was more to it
than just the hard labor.

01:02:31.590 --> 01:02:33.830
It played another role, right?

01:02:33.830 --> 01:02:38.830
In the community, but I'm
curious how that might have

01:02:38.980 --> 01:02:41.380
fed some of the things
that you think about too?

01:02:43.033 --> 01:02:45.283
- Well working hard that
way with somebody is

01:02:47.762 --> 01:02:51.467
well there's a certain analogy to dancing.

01:02:52.780 --> 01:02:57.023
You work, crude work, to go well,

01:02:58.870 --> 01:03:02.293
it has to be done by
people who know each other,

01:03:02.293 --> 01:03:04.170
and know how to anticipate each other,

01:03:04.170 --> 01:03:06.320
know each others moves, and so on,

01:03:06.320 --> 01:03:09.453
so you begin to know
those people intimately,

01:03:10.730 --> 01:03:13.940
and that, you know, after
you've lost some of 'em,

01:03:13.940 --> 01:03:17.293
they've gone on, it become
very dear knowledge.

01:03:19.860 --> 01:03:24.860
But also, these situations
in which people do work

01:03:25.220 --> 01:03:30.143
that can be really miserable,
become very good talkers.

01:03:31.780 --> 01:03:34.950
And I don't know how a writer
could have had a better

01:03:36.340 --> 01:03:38.553
background of that kind than I did.

01:03:39.610 --> 01:03:42.440
To listen to the talk, to take part in it,

01:03:42.440 --> 01:03:47.054
in tobacco barns, strippin'
rooms, tobacco pastures,

01:03:47.054 --> 01:03:50.993
around the water jug, I'd
roll ends, those things,

01:03:52.546 --> 01:03:56.020
I've just heard wonderful talk,

01:03:56.020 --> 01:03:58.820
by people who really couldn't talk.

01:03:58.820 --> 01:04:01.350
Not necessarily educated people,

01:04:01.350 --> 01:04:05.280
people who really knew how to
talk for their own amusement,

01:04:05.280 --> 01:04:07.193
and to amuse everybody else.

01:04:08.690 --> 01:04:13.690
And so, you have this odd convergence

01:04:14.481 --> 01:04:17.113
in this really hard cruel work.

01:04:18.180 --> 01:04:23.180
And that is the convergence
of suffering and pleasure.

01:04:26.411 --> 01:04:29.933
I'll tell you a story, we had a man who,

01:04:32.370 --> 01:04:34.606
was a part of our,

01:04:34.606 --> 01:04:38.580
a part of our crew, and
he had a childhood disease

01:04:38.580 --> 01:04:41.492
that crippled him, he
always called it polio

01:04:41.492 --> 01:04:43.243
because he didn't wanna
explain what it really was.

01:04:44.660 --> 01:04:49.660
But he was a small man, probably
in the days of his vigor,

01:04:52.450 --> 01:04:56.500
he never weighed over 120, 25 pounds,

01:04:56.500 --> 01:04:58.543
but he did as much work as anybody.

01:04:59.800 --> 01:05:04.800
He was, everybody admired him, you know?

01:05:07.794 --> 01:05:10.883
And in 1973, I had a photographer friend

01:05:14.740 --> 01:05:19.740
named James Baker Hall,
who turned up at home

01:05:21.280 --> 01:05:24.103
during tobacco cutting
time, and I said, you know,

01:05:25.291 --> 01:05:27.711
while you're here, you might
like to take some pictures

01:05:27.711 --> 01:05:30.253
of the tobacco harvest,
and he took a whole set.

01:05:31.645 --> 01:05:33.463
He was a good photographer
and they were good pictures,

01:05:34.321 --> 01:05:35.160
and this is a set of pictures that

01:05:35.160 --> 01:05:37.310
could not be reproduced now.

01:05:37.310 --> 01:05:40.743
I mean the age of those pictures,

01:05:41.937 --> 01:05:46.163
what 40 years ago, is gone, it is over.

01:05:48.930 --> 01:05:53.930
But then we exhibited those
pictures in the county seat,

01:05:54.850 --> 01:05:57.483
at an event, and,

01:05:59.540 --> 01:06:02.420
Eddie, my friend I'm telling you about,

01:06:02.420 --> 01:06:04.410
sort of took up a position there,

01:06:04.410 --> 01:06:06.270
a lot of people were
looking at those pictures

01:06:06.270 --> 01:06:08.320
not knowing what they were seeing.

01:06:08.320 --> 01:06:11.510
Lot of old farmers were there too,

01:06:11.510 --> 01:06:15.133
and would look at those pictures
and cry, 'cause they knew.

01:06:16.390 --> 01:06:19.540
But Eddie took his
station, and I heard him

01:06:19.540 --> 01:06:22.643
tell a bunch of people, he said,

01:06:24.158 --> 01:06:25.160
you're wondering where you could do that

01:06:25.160 --> 01:06:26.603
and keep it from being hard?

01:06:27.690 --> 01:06:30.560
Said it was hard work,

01:06:30.560 --> 01:06:32.823
but you wouldn't believe the fun we had.

01:06:34.900 --> 01:06:36.293
I loved him, you know.

01:06:37.590 --> 01:06:40.033
- Alright, I'm gonna ask
you one last question.

01:06:41.225 --> 01:06:44.323
Which is, I heard this last night,

01:06:46.061 --> 01:06:48.562
Wendell had dinner with
a number of students

01:06:48.562 --> 01:06:51.163
from the Sustainable Farm
Project here at Yale,

01:06:52.040 --> 01:06:54.310
and my students in Timothy Dwight College,

01:06:54.310 --> 01:06:56.193
we had a good time last night.

01:06:57.554 --> 01:07:00.393
And you gave some advice
to future farmers.

01:07:01.369 --> 01:07:02.592
(laughing)

01:07:02.592 --> 01:07:04.810
Or you gave some advice to
them about being a farmer,

01:07:04.810 --> 01:07:09.070
and I think maybe, given
that I've heard it,

01:07:09.070 --> 01:07:12.090
it might be useful to
give that advice now.

01:07:12.090 --> 01:07:13.240
- You mean don't do it?

01:07:14.293 --> 01:07:17.168
(laughing)

01:07:17.168 --> 01:07:19.783
Yeah, yeah, I was tellin' 'em,

01:07:21.220 --> 01:07:25.750
I had a very naive idea of
what was to be a writer.

01:07:25.750 --> 01:07:30.170
I didn't anticipate at first that

01:07:31.010 --> 01:07:32.800
publishing a book would cause

01:07:32.800 --> 01:07:34.883
you to be asked to make a speech.

01:07:36.360 --> 01:07:39.125
I found that shocking and then,

01:07:39.125 --> 01:07:41.580
(laughing)

01:07:41.580 --> 01:07:44.503
remunerated, I'd penned on it for a while.

01:07:47.340 --> 01:07:52.340
But also, after I published the
Unsettling of America, 1977,

01:07:55.380 --> 01:07:58.410
I began to get letters from people

01:08:00.668 --> 01:08:04.480
at the extreme end of the
range of these letters,

01:08:04.480 --> 01:08:07.870
people would say I'm sick of my work,

01:08:07.870 --> 01:08:09.360
I know it's not good work,

01:08:09.360 --> 01:08:11.830
I know it's not doing good to the world,

01:08:11.830 --> 01:08:16.830
I'm gonna quit it, and
sell out, and buy a farm.

01:08:20.808 --> 01:08:23.423
And that really did set me back.

01:08:24.630 --> 01:08:27.970
Because I saw that I'd
assumed a responsibility

01:08:27.970 --> 01:08:30.553
that I didn't intend to take on at all.

01:08:32.030 --> 01:08:36.577
So I began to tell some
people just don't do that.

01:08:36.577 --> 01:08:37.410
(laughing)

01:08:37.410 --> 01:08:40.393
If you're 45 or 50 years old,

01:08:42.210 --> 01:08:46.143
and you've never farmed, don't do it.

01:08:47.171 --> 01:08:49.421
(laughing)

01:08:52.830 --> 01:08:57.830
And so I have, you can't advise
people, you really can't.

01:08:59.920 --> 01:09:02.460
You can't tell people
what they ought to do

01:09:03.342 --> 01:09:05.610
if you don't know them, you
don't know where they are,

01:09:05.610 --> 01:09:08.560
you don't really know what
they're talking about.

01:09:08.560 --> 01:09:11.080
But people do get the
feeling of being lost

01:09:11.080 --> 01:09:14.483
and bewildered and they write to somebody

01:09:14.483 --> 01:09:19.006
that sounds like he's not
bewildered, which is a front.

01:09:19.006 --> 01:09:21.100
(laughing)

01:09:21.100 --> 01:09:25.310
- But yesterday you had a
wonderful visit at the Yale farm,

01:09:25.310 --> 01:09:28.150
where it was Friday, and I
think you wouldn't be here

01:09:29.769 --> 01:09:32.043
if it wasn't for the Yale farm
and the Yale Food Project,

01:09:32.043 --> 01:09:34.300
and Mark Bomford's work, and Jackie Lewin,

01:09:34.300 --> 01:09:35.700
so there's other ways, right,

01:09:35.700 --> 01:09:37.910
to bring this in to the conversation.

01:09:37.910 --> 01:09:42.193
- So, I've developed ways
to answer the other people,

01:09:43.230 --> 01:09:46.673
young people, who have a good job,

01:09:47.790 --> 01:09:51.713
in town, well I say, go
ahead, but keep your job.

01:09:53.010 --> 01:09:58.010
It's possible to think of
selling your house in town

01:09:59.940 --> 01:10:01.600
maybe you get a good deal, you know,

01:10:01.600 --> 01:10:06.190
and buy a house with some
land around it in the country,

01:10:06.190 --> 01:10:09.363
and you'd be in the same fix, essentially.

01:10:10.920 --> 01:10:13.740
And you don't, if you've
got your town job,

01:10:13.740 --> 01:10:15.183
you don't have to farm it.

01:10:17.750 --> 01:10:19.710
You're not economically dependent on it,

01:10:19.710 --> 01:10:22.110
so all you need to do
is mow it once a year

01:10:22.110 --> 01:10:24.250
to keep the bushes from taking it,

01:10:24.250 --> 01:10:26.920
keep it in grass, and
it doesn't do a thing

01:10:26.920 --> 01:10:28.623
but lie there and get better.

01:10:30.050 --> 01:10:34.053
And then as you feel
ambitious, make a garden,

01:10:34.890 --> 01:10:39.759
get a few chickens, get a pig.

01:10:39.759 --> 01:10:41.736
(laughing)

01:10:41.736 --> 01:10:43.686
A few sheep, but a build a fence first.

01:10:45.043 --> 01:10:47.610
(laughing)

01:10:47.610 --> 01:10:51.910
There are ways to make this transition.

01:10:51.910 --> 01:10:55.070
And there are ways for people
who don't know how to farm

01:10:55.070 --> 01:10:57.940
who've never lived in such a place,

01:10:57.940 --> 01:11:02.940
have no experience to get experience,

01:11:03.040 --> 01:11:07.600
to get experienced, and to get help

01:11:07.600 --> 01:11:10.740
from asking neighbors
from people who know,

01:11:10.740 --> 01:11:13.700
people who know wanna tell usually.

01:11:13.700 --> 01:11:18.700
And it can be tried
out, it can also be quit

01:11:20.093 --> 01:11:23.616
if you haven't put everything
in jeopardy, you see.

01:11:23.616 --> 01:11:24.937
- Right, what I'm saying,

01:11:24.937 --> 01:11:25.770
and you have brought this forward too,

01:11:25.770 --> 01:11:28.350
is there are a number of
models, like you've just said,

01:11:28.350 --> 01:11:31.000
and I'm really just trying
to give a shout out here

01:11:31.000 --> 01:11:34.613
to our Yale farm and what
they have done for Yale.

01:11:35.607 --> 01:11:37.857
(clapping)

01:11:39.319 --> 01:11:43.480
- Well this is what I
started, was meaning,

01:11:43.480 --> 01:11:45.200
an example, what I was meaning when

01:11:45.200 --> 01:11:48.610
I was talking about the university.

01:11:48.610 --> 01:11:52.490
An assortment of disciplines
are represented over there.

01:11:52.490 --> 01:11:54.840
- And also the community
supported agriculture,

01:11:54.840 --> 01:11:57.120
we have Massaro Farm out in Woodbridge,

01:11:57.120 --> 01:11:59.050
and Steve Muno is here,

01:11:59.050 --> 01:12:01.320
and that's fantastic what they have done.

01:12:01.320 --> 01:12:04.433
- Well, we subscribe to a CSA ourselves.

01:12:06.019 --> 01:12:08.420
As we've shrunk our garden some.

01:12:08.420 --> 01:12:09.253
But,

01:12:11.556 --> 01:12:12.840
because of age and wear,

01:12:12.840 --> 01:12:17.840
but all this is absolutely important.

01:12:17.910 --> 01:12:19.710
I'm glad that we got around to this.

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:25.810
Urban gardening, urban agriculture,

01:12:25.810 --> 01:12:29.640
those things, subscribing to CSA's,

01:12:29.640 --> 01:12:31.393
going to the farmers markets,

01:12:32.583 --> 01:12:36.847
all those things are of
urgent importance because

01:12:38.600 --> 01:12:42.860
farmers are not anybody's
constituents anymore.

01:12:42.860 --> 01:12:44.327
There are not enough of 'em.

01:12:45.946 --> 01:12:47.997
And I think all the thoughtful ones

01:12:47.997 --> 01:12:49.920
who want agriculture to survive

01:12:49.920 --> 01:12:52.450
and themselves and their
families along with it,

01:12:52.450 --> 01:12:57.040
know they've got to have
informed urban allies.

01:12:57.040 --> 01:13:00.570
And so there is a kind
of urban agrarianism

01:13:00.570 --> 01:13:03.100
that's developing in the cities,

01:13:03.100 --> 01:13:08.100
it's not knowledgeable
and refined enough yet,

01:13:08.270 --> 01:13:10.073
but it's going in that direction.

01:13:10.930 --> 01:13:12.450
And Yale's School of Forestry
and Environmental Studies

01:13:12.450 --> 01:13:15.750
has urban ecology and
tremendous planting of trees

01:13:15.750 --> 01:13:17.957
here in New Haven with--

01:13:17.957 --> 01:13:19.620
- Well when you said for urban forestry,

01:13:19.620 --> 01:13:22.235
it's a shame to throw away a good tree

01:13:22.235 --> 01:13:24.580
just because it happened
to blow down in town.

01:13:24.580 --> 01:13:26.100
- Right.

01:13:26.100 --> 01:13:28.350
(laughing)

01:13:30.590 --> 01:13:31.653
- Urban logging.

01:13:32.617 --> 01:13:34.867
(laughing)

01:13:37.500 --> 01:13:41.370
- Well Wendell I hoped you'd maybe close

01:13:42.500 --> 01:13:44.430
our time together this afternoon

01:13:44.430 --> 01:13:48.323
by reading a poem that
you'd like us to hear.

01:13:49.870 --> 01:13:51.473
On this particular occasion,

01:13:52.812 --> 01:13:53.723
I think you picked out something.

01:13:56.740 --> 01:14:01.214
- You had told me that, and
I decided I'd read three.

01:14:01.214 --> 01:14:03.229
(laughing)

01:14:03.229 --> 01:14:05.092
(clapping)

01:14:05.092 --> 01:14:06.592
- Thank you Jesus!

01:14:07.842 --> 01:14:10.092
(clapping)

01:14:12.820 --> 01:14:15.130
- We were in a quandary, Jeff and I,

01:14:15.130 --> 01:14:17.390
about how we would get a book.

01:14:17.390 --> 01:14:20.110
We went down to his office and Mary Evelyn

01:14:20.110 --> 01:14:22.670
had put a book down there
for me to sign, you see,

01:14:22.670 --> 01:14:25.515
so this was all divinely arranged.

01:14:25.515 --> 01:14:27.765
(laughing)

01:14:29.250 --> 01:14:31.720
This is a book called This Day,

01:14:31.720 --> 01:14:36.650
and it's a collection of all
the so called sabbath poems,

01:14:36.650 --> 01:14:41.093
until now, and as a,

01:14:42.690 --> 01:14:44.040
let's see what I called it,

01:14:45.190 --> 01:14:50.190
as a preface, I used two
new poems from this year,

01:14:50.740 --> 01:14:55.233
this sabbaths 1979 to 2012.

01:14:58.200 --> 01:15:01.850
And I'll read one of those poems,

01:15:01.850 --> 01:15:06.333
and then a little short one,
and then a concluding one.

01:15:08.860 --> 01:15:13.860
Times will come as they must,
by necessity or his wish.

01:15:14.860 --> 01:15:19.130
When he leaves, his
enclosure and his window,

01:15:19.130 --> 01:15:22.240
his home scape of house and garden,

01:15:22.240 --> 01:15:26.340
barn and pasture, the incarnate life

01:15:26.340 --> 01:15:30.023
of his desire, thought, and daily work.

01:15:31.010 --> 01:15:33.200
His grazing animals look up to

01:15:33.200 --> 01:15:36.573
watch in silence as he departs.

01:15:37.441 --> 01:15:40.930
He sets out in times without even a path

01:15:40.930 --> 01:15:44.430
or any guidance other than
knowledge of the place

01:15:44.430 --> 01:15:49.430
and himself as they were
in time already past.

01:15:50.150 --> 01:15:55.150
He goes among trees, climbing
again the one hill of his life

01:15:56.650 --> 01:16:01.650
with his hand full of words
he goes into the wordless,

01:16:01.700 --> 01:16:05.890
wording it barely in time as he passes.

01:16:05.890 --> 01:16:10.890
One by one he places
words, balancing on each,

01:16:11.420 --> 01:16:15.750
as on a small stone in the swift flow,

01:16:15.750 --> 01:16:20.633
in his anxious patience
until the next arrival,

01:16:21.973 --> 01:16:24.540
until he has come at last again

01:16:24.540 --> 01:16:27.483
into presentment of the real,

01:16:28.871 --> 01:16:33.250
the holy real, in it's grand composure.

01:16:33.250 --> 01:16:37.720
For which as before, he knows no word,

01:16:37.720 --> 01:16:41.330
and here again, he must stop.

01:16:41.330 --> 01:16:46.060
Here by luck or grace he may find rest,

01:16:46.060 --> 01:16:49.390
which he has been seeking all along.

01:16:49.390 --> 01:16:54.390
Sometimes by the times
flaws and his own he fails.

01:16:56.330 --> 01:16:59.300
And then by luck or grace

01:16:59.300 --> 01:17:03.480
he will be given another day to try again,

01:17:03.480 --> 01:17:08.480
to go maybe yet farther
before again he must stop.

01:17:09.840 --> 01:17:14.840
He is a gatherer of fragments,
a cobbler of pieces,

01:17:15.590 --> 01:17:20.340
piece by piece he tells
a story without end,

01:17:20.340 --> 01:17:25.340
for in the time of this
world, no end can come.

01:17:25.870 --> 01:17:29.710
It is the story of eternities shining,

01:17:29.710 --> 01:17:34.290
much shadowed, much put off, in time.

01:17:34.290 --> 01:17:38.173
And time, however long, falls short.

01:17:42.333 --> 01:17:44.583
(clapping)

01:17:49.871 --> 01:17:51.490
And this is a poem from
last summers drought.

01:17:51.490 --> 01:17:53.623
We had a really,

01:17:56.620 --> 01:17:59.210
very severe drought last summer.

01:17:59.210 --> 01:18:02.270
No, no, summer before last.

01:18:02.270 --> 01:18:03.403
2012.

01:18:05.530 --> 01:18:10.530
After the long weeks, when
the heat curled the leaves,

01:18:10.740 --> 01:18:15.740
and the air thirsted,
comes a morning after rain,

01:18:16.200 --> 01:18:18.550
cool and bright,

01:18:18.550 --> 01:18:23.550
the leaves uncurl, the
pastures began again to grow.

01:18:24.730 --> 01:18:28.470
The animals and the birds rejoice,

01:18:28.470 --> 01:18:33.017
if tonight the world ends,
we'll of had this day.

01:18:34.645 --> 01:18:36.895
(clapping)

01:18:41.880 --> 01:18:44.080
See I'm working toward
what I'll settle for.

01:18:45.313 --> 01:18:47.050
(laughing)

01:18:47.050 --> 01:18:50.250
Now this is a poem, that to my surprise

01:18:50.250 --> 01:18:52.090
is a character I've written about before

01:18:52.090 --> 01:18:55.450
called the Mad Farmer,
enters into this sequence,

01:18:55.450 --> 01:18:59.093
or this, not a sequence,
it's a series of poems,

01:19:00.480 --> 01:19:03.390
and the Mad Farmer is
a character I've used

01:19:03.390 --> 01:19:08.080
to accommodate extravagance.

01:19:08.080 --> 01:19:12.073
He's mad sometimes in the sense of angry,

01:19:13.230 --> 01:19:16.780
he's never mad in the sense of crazy,

01:19:16.780 --> 01:19:19.360
but he's mad in the sense that

01:19:19.360 --> 01:19:24.360
in a crazy time, somebody as
sane as he is looks crazy.

01:19:26.579 --> 01:19:28.829
(laughing)

01:19:30.860 --> 01:19:32.423
And this is a Christmas poem.

01:19:34.022 --> 01:19:36.272
(laughing)

01:19:37.330 --> 01:19:41.610
As a child, the Mad Farmer saw easily,

01:19:41.610 --> 01:19:46.000
the vision of heavens
Christ born in a stable.

01:19:46.000 --> 01:19:49.870
The brilliant star
stopped in the high dark,

01:19:49.870 --> 01:19:54.790
the sheltered beasts standing silently by.

01:19:54.790 --> 01:19:59.510
He knows the beasts, he
is himself a shepherd,

01:19:59.510 --> 01:20:04.190
and still more clearly
by the gift of a moment,

01:20:04.190 --> 01:20:08.760
he sees the shepherds on
their cold hill by night.

01:20:08.760 --> 01:20:13.240
The sky flying suddenly
open over their heads,

01:20:13.240 --> 01:20:16.890
the light of very heaven
falling upon them,

01:20:16.890 --> 01:20:20.870
the angels descending, slowly as snow,

01:20:20.870 --> 01:20:25.240
their singing filling
far and wide the dark

01:20:25.240 --> 01:20:29.450
on earth, peace good will.

01:20:29.450 --> 01:20:33.340
The vision, the gift, only of moments

01:20:33.340 --> 01:20:37.060
he has kept in his eyes, in his heart.

01:20:37.060 --> 01:20:41.200
He knows how it passes, how it fades,

01:20:41.200 --> 01:20:45.173
how it stays, how far we have drawn away.

01:20:46.143 --> 01:20:51.143
He thinks of distance,
the hard hungry journey

01:20:51.240 --> 01:20:56.240
of a foolish man, a pilgrim in
the foreshadow of apocalypse

01:20:57.400 --> 01:21:00.410
toward the almost forgotten light

01:21:00.410 --> 01:21:04.940
far beyond the polluted
river, the blasted mountains,

01:21:04.940 --> 01:21:08.640
the killed children, the bombed villages,

01:21:08.640 --> 01:21:12.890
jaunted already by the
hurting bodies of their dead.

01:21:12.890 --> 01:21:17.890
Some of the past he dreads as
if it has not yet happened.

01:21:19.100 --> 01:21:23.083
From present portent, he
fears the time to come,

01:21:23.920 --> 01:21:27.870
beyond and beyond is
the shepherds startling,

01:21:27.870 --> 01:21:32.870
ever staying light, no creature
of his slow minded kind

01:21:34.220 --> 01:21:39.113
may ever stand in that
light again, he sets out.

01:21:40.154 --> 01:21:42.404
(clapping)

01:21:53.018 --> 01:21:55.082
So we're gettin' off.

01:21:55.082 --> 01:21:56.082
- Thank you.

01:22:05.102 --> 01:22:06.769
- Thank you Wendell.

01:22:09.673 --> 01:22:11.006
- Thank you sir.

01:22:12.881 --> 01:22:15.131
(clapping)

01:22:28.035 --> 01:22:29.920
(laughing)

01:22:29.920 --> 01:22:32.464
- You are all polite, but
you oughta be more critical.

01:22:32.464 --> 01:22:34.730
(laughing)

01:22:34.730 --> 01:22:35.743
- Alright, okay.

01:22:38.045 --> 01:22:39.659
- Oh boy.

01:22:39.659 --> 01:22:41.909
(laughing)

